All...

Some while ago I posted a request for a solaris equivalent of the tru64 unix
tools dxterneltuner or sysconfig for checking kernel parameter settings.

Many thanks for all who replied:

Rainer_Freis@santix.de
Stuart Whitby [swhitby@legato.com]
jreed@wukon.appliedtheory.com
Larye D. Parkins [larye@selway.umt.edu]
Aleksey Tsalolikhin [atsaloli@earthlink.net]


Kernel parameters
=================
man -s4 system
man modinfo

Probably the one you're after is sysdef.

"ndd" works to view tcpip parameters.  For others, I think a combination of
modload and sysdef will give you info. I've only played with it a bit. I do
know that if the module
that the parameters refer to has not been loaded since boot you won't see
the new values - that's why you use the modload command first.

Here is some useful data on tuning the TCP/IP stack parameters (what I have
experience with under Solaris).
http://www.rvs.uni-hannover.de/people/voeckler/tune/EN/tune.html



Excellent book:
===============
"Sun Performance and Tuning" from Adrian Cockcroft (also includes tuneable
kernel parameters and their default values.

Always run catman once new manual pages are installed - man -k is second
only to the wheel (the first users of the wheel complained about the
documentation too).

Check out http://docs.sun.com/ab2

Try docs.sun.com.  Or get an account on sunsolve.sun.com.  Both are
really good sysadmin resources for Sun systems.  There's also a 
Solaris admin mailing list in a similar vein to this one.  Send mail
to majordomo@sunmanagers.ececs.uc.edu with "subscribe sun-managers"
in the body.  Searching sunworld.com has some pretty good articles 
as well, and searchable archives.

The red book (Nemeth) is quite good, and it does cover Solaris in addition
to the other OSes.  Anything by Cockroft is good too, for  performance
tuning Solaris boxes.  




The original post is shown at the end of this message.

Regards - Tony



